[{"id":"29471","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following is an ore of mercury?</span></p>","option_1":"Bauxite","option_2":"Magnetite","option_3":"Cinnabar","option_4":"Malachite","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Cinnabar.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Cinnabar (mercury sulphide, HgS) is the principal ore from which mercury metal is extracted. Bauxite is an ore of aluminium, magnetite is an ore of iron, and malachite is an ore of copper. This distinction between ores of different metals is a recurring static GK topic in SSC exams.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29472","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The Durgapur steel plant was set up in collaboration with the government of the _____.</span></p>","option_1":"France","option_2":"United Kingdom","option_3":"United States of America","option_4":"Russia","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - United Kingdom.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Durgapur Steel Plant (West Bengal), one of India's first public sector steel plants, was set up in collaboration with the United Kingdom. For comparison, Bhilai was set up with Soviet Russia's help and Rourkela with West Germany's help - a classic SSC static GK grouping (plant-country pairs) that is frequently asked.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29473","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following is NOT the division of the Himalaya?</span></p>","option_1":"Himadri","option_2":"Himachal","option_3":"Shiwalik","option_4":"Aravali","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Aravali.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Himalayas are conventionally divided (north to south) into Himadri (Greater Himalaya), Himachal (Lesser/Middle Himalaya), and Shiwalik (Outer Himalaya/Sub-Himalaya). The Aravali range is a completely separate, much older mountain range located in Rajasthan/Gujarat/Haryana/Delhi, unrelated to the Himalayan system.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29474","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which metal was unknown to the Harappan people?</span></p>","option_1":"Iron","option_2":"Gold","option_3":"Silver","option_4":"Copper","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Iron.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Harappan (Indus Valley) Civilisation belonged to the Bronze Age and used copper, bronze (copper+tin), gold, and silver for tools and ornaments, but iron was not known to them. Iron use in India is associated with the later Vedic/Iron Age period, well after the decline of the Harappan civilisation.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29475","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">How do plants primarily get rid of excess water?</span></p>","option_1":"By transpiration","option_2":"By shedding their roots","option_3":"By secreting through the stems","option_4":"By storing water in fruits","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - By transpiration.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Transpiration is the process by which plants lose excess water in the form of water vapour, mainly through stomata present on the surface of leaves. It also helps in the upward movement of water and minerals (transpiration pull) and in cooling the plant.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29476","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following statements regarding the fundamental rights of the Indian constitution is correct?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>I. Article 26 is related to Freedom from Taxation for Promotion of a Religion</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>II. Article 27 is related to freedom to manage religious affairs.</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I Nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Neither I Nor II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Both statements are swapped/incorrect. Article 26 actually deals with the freedom to manage religious affairs (right of every religious denomination to establish and maintain institutions, manage its own affairs in matters of religion, and own/administer property). Article 27 deals with freedom from payment of taxes for promotion of a particular religion - i.e., no person can be compelled to pay any taxes for the promotion of a particular religion. Since the statements have been interchanged, neither is correct as given.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29477","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">The last subject mentioned in the concurrent list of the Indian Constitution is numbered _____.</span></p>","option_1":"34","option_2":"42","option_3":"47","option_4":"41","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 47.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Concurrent List (List III) in the Seventh Schedule of the Indian Constitution originally contained 47 subjects (entries), with entry 47 being the last one (\"Jurisdiction and powers of all courts, except the Supreme Court, with respect to any of the matters in this list\"). Subsequent amendments have added a few more entries, but 47 is the classic exam-relevant figure for the original count.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29478","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">'Chawng Laizawn' is a popular dance of which of the following Indian state/Union Territory?</span></p>","option_1":"Mizoram","option_2":"Assam","option_3":"Ladakh","option_4":"Chhattisgarh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Mizoram.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Chawng Laizawn (also spelt Chawnglaizawn) is a folk dance of Mizoram, performed by young Mizo girls, usually during festive occasions. Regional/state-wise folk dances form an important and frequently tested static GK area in SSC exams.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29479","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which Indian state is famous for the folk dance called 'Kajri'?</span></p>","option_1":"Assam","option_2":"Punjab","option_3":"Maharashtra","option_4":"Uttar Pradesh","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Uttar Pradesh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Kajri is a folk song-and-dance form associated with the rainy season (monsoon), popular in Uttar Pradesh, particularly around Mirzapur and Banaras, and also in parts of Bihar. It is traditionally sung by women to celebrate the arrival of the monsoon.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29480","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">'Ganga Dussehra' is celebrated in the month of _____.</span></p>","option_1":"January","option_2":"October","option_3":"November","option_4":"June","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - June.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Ganga Dussehra, which commemorates the descent of the river Ganga to earth, is celebrated in the Hindu month of Jyeshtha, which typically falls in May-June (most commonly June) as per the Gregorian calendar. It is celebrated ten days before Ganga Saptami.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29481","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">As per census 2011, which among the following state had the highest population?</span></p>","option_1":"Rajasthan","option_2":"West Bengal","option_3":"Karnataka","option_4":"Tamil Nadu","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - West Bengal.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>As per Census 2011, among the given options, West Bengal had the highest population (about 9.13 crore), ahead of Tamil Nadu (~7.21 crore), Rajasthan (~6.85 crore) and Karnataka (~6.11 crore). Note that Uttar Pradesh is the most populous state in India overall, but it is not among the given options here.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29482","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In the context of Chandrayaan-III mission, the SHAPE payload was used to _____.</span></p>","option_1":"detect moonquakes","option_2":"study Earth's spectral and polarimetric properties from lunar orbit","option_3":"measure thermal conductivity from lunar surface","option_4":"study the plasma density on lunar surface","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - study Earth's spectral and polarimetric properties from lunar orbit.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>SHAPE (Spectro-polarimetry of HAbitable Planet Earth) was a payload aboard the Propulsion Module of Chandrayaan-3, designed to study the spectral and polarimetric characteristics of Earth as seen from lunar orbit - useful for future research on identifying habitable exoplanets. Moonquake detection was done by the ILSA instrument on the Vikram lander, and thermal conductivity was measured by ChaSTE.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29483","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">1 kJ = _____ J</span></p>","option_1":"10","option_2":"100","option_3":"1000","option_4":"10000","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 1000.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The prefix 'kilo' denotes a factor of 1000 in the SI system, so 1 kilojoule (kJ) equals 1000 joules (J). This is a basic unit-conversion fact frequently tested in SSC science sections.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29484","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Who wrote Hind Swaraj in 1909?</span></p>","option_1":"Ravindra Nath Tagore","option_2":"Gopal Krishna Gokhale","option_3":"Bal Gangadher Tilak","option_4":"Mahatma Gandhi","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Mahatma Gandhi.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Hind Swaraj (or Indian Home Rule) was written by Mahatma Gandhi in 1909, originally in Gujarati, during his voyage from London to South Africa. In it, Gandhi criticised Western/modern civilisation and outlined his vision of Swaraj (self-rule) based on truth and non-violence.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29485","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which organization developed the SCOT mission in January 2025?</span></p>","option_1":"Skyroot Aerospace","option_2":"Agnikul Cosmos","option_3":"Digantara","option_4":"Pixxel","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Digantara.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>SCOT (Space Camera for Object Tracking) is a space-based surveillance mission developed by Digantara, an Indian space-situational-awareness (space surveillance and tracking) start-up, launched in January 2025 to track and monitor objects in orbit, including space debris and satellites. This distinguishes it from Skyroot and Agnikul (both launch-vehicle companies) and Pixxel (an earth-observation/imaging satellite company).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29486","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">What is the name of the Large Language Model (LLM) developed by CoRover.ai?</span></p>","option_1":"IndiaGPT","option_2":"BharatAI","option_3":"BharatGPT","option_4":"DeshGPT","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - BharatGPT.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>BharatGPT is the indigenous Large Language Model (LLM) developed by CoRover.ai, designed to support multiple Indian languages and power conversational AI applications for Indian users and government/enterprise use cases.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29487","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">In the context of population change, if the birth rate is _____ than death rate, the population will _____.</span></p>","option_1":"less, increase","option_2":"less, decrease","option_3":"more, be same","option_4":"less, be same","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - less, decrease.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Population change is governed by the relationship between birth rate and death rate. If the birth rate is less than the death rate, more people are dying than being born, so the overall population will decrease (negative natural growth rate). Conversely, if birth rate exceeds death rate, population increases.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29488","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following economic activity is NOT included in the primary sector?</span></p>","option_1":"Fishing","option_2":"Mining","option_3":"Manufacturing","option_4":"Forestry","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Manufacturing.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The primary sector covers activities directly dependent on natural resources - agriculture, fishing, mining, and forestry. Manufacturing, which involves converting raw materials into finished goods, belongs to the secondary sector, not the primary sector. This three-sector classification (primary, secondary, tertiary) is a fundamental economics concept tested in SSC exams.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29489","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Who is the author of the book 'When Love Came Calling'?</span></p>","option_1":"Aruni Kashyap","option_2":"Preeti Shenoy","option_3":"Chetan Bhagat","option_4":"Tanya Selvaratnam","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Preeti Shenoy.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>'When Love Came Calling' is a novel written by the popular Indian author Preeti Shenoy, known for her contemporary fiction dealing with relationships and emotional themes. Books-and-authors is a recurring static GK area, and this question was left unanswered by the candidate.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29490","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following sector is NOT covered under Production Linked Incentive (PLI) scheme?</span></p>","option_1":"Manufacturing of Medical Devices","option_2":"Leather Industry","option_3":"Automobiles and Auto Components","option_4":"Drones and Drone Components","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Leather Industry.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Production Linked Incentive (PLI) scheme currently covers 14 key sectors including medical devices, automobiles and auto components, drones and drone components, telecom, pharmaceuticals, textiles, food processing, etc., aimed at boosting domestic manufacturing. The leather industry is not among the sectors covered under the PLI scheme.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29491","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following is a type of service in volleyball?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>I. Jump and Serve</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>II. Floating service</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>In volleyball, common types of serves include the underarm serve, overhand (tennis) serve, jump serve, and the floating (float) serve, in which the ball is hit without spin so that it moves unpredictably in the air. Both the jump serve and the floating service listed here are recognised, valid types of volleyball service.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29492","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">How many distinct temples are there in the Dilwara Temples complex?</span></p>","option_1":"2","option_2":"3","option_3":"5","option_4":"7","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - 5.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>The Dilwara Temples, located near Mount Abu in Rajasthan, are a group of five Jain temples renowned for their exquisite marble carvings: Vimal Vasahi, Luna Vasahi, Pittalhar, Parshvanath, and Mahavir Swami temples. This question was left unanswered by the candidate.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29493","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">What is molecular mass?</span></p>","option_1":"The total number of atoms in a molecule","option_2":"The sum of the valencies of the atoms in a molecule","option_3":"The sum of the atomic masses of all atoms in a molecule","option_4":"The volume occupied by a molecule","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - The sum of the atomic masses of all atoms in a molecule.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Molecular mass (or molecular weight) is defined as the sum of the atomic masses of all the atoms present in a molecule of a substance, expressed in atomic mass units (amu). For example, the molecular mass of water (H2O) = 2(1) + 16 = 18 amu.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29494","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">Which of the following is correct regarding Gitam?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>I. It is the simplest type of composition.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>II. It is very simple in construction, with an easy and melodious flow of music.</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Gitam is considered the most basic and simplest form of composition in Carnatic music, typically taught to beginners. It has a simple structure with an easy, flowing melody and little rhythmic or lyrical complexity, making both given statements about it correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"29495","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"764","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span style=\"font-weight:bold\">EnviStats India 2025: Environment Statistics has been released by which of the following?</span></p>","option_1":"Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation","option_2":"Ministry of Environment, Forest and Climate Change","option_3":"Ministry of Home Affairs","option_4":"Ministry of Social Justice and Empowerment","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":"<style> *{overflow-wrap: break-word;} </style> <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Ministry of Statistics &amp; Programme Implementation.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt;background-color:#ffffff\"><span>EnviStats India is an annual publication released by the Ministry of Statistics and Programme Implementation (MoSPI), compiling environment-related statistics across themes such as environmental pollution, biodiversity, forestry, and natural resources, in line with the Framework for Development of Environment Statistics (FDES).</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]